1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,500
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,000
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:11,017 --> 00:00:11,600
PROFESSOR: OK.

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:16,580
So just to remind you--

6
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:22,160
so last time, we looked at--

7
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:26,020
we had the motivating
example that we had a graph.

8
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:31,840
We had a function f defined
on some interval from a to b.

9
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:35,960
And it was going inside
the real numbers.

10
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:38,660
And it started
off negative here.

11
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:40,520
This is f of a.

12
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:42,400
This is negative.

13
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:47,000
And it ended up
positive, f of b.

14
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,880
And we wanted to say--

15
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:50,510
and this was a
continuous function.

16
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:04,450
And we wanted to say
that there was some c.

17
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:16,510
So we wanted to show that
there's a c between a and b

18
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:21,170
so that the value
at c is equal to 0.

19
00:01:21,170 --> 00:01:23,220
So this was the
intermediate value theorem.

20
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:38,790
And so this here was
a motivating example

21
00:01:38,790 --> 00:01:43,810
for trying to understand,
what property of R do we need?

22
00:01:43,810 --> 00:02:10,650
So what property of R do we
need to make this rigorous?

23
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:13,790
So that was the
motivating example.

24
00:02:13,790 --> 00:02:19,530
And so in order to
make this rigorous,

25
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:27,050
well, then we need to
concept of that R--

26
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:31,370
we need to understand
the following statement,

27
00:02:31,370 --> 00:02:42,600
that R is a complete
ordered field.

28
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:50,610
And a field here--
so just briefly,

29
00:02:50,610 --> 00:03:07,560
fields here was a set with
two operations, a plus

30
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:09,820
and what we called
multiplication.

31
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:30,130
So each of these two
operations have a 5 property.

32
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:39,040
And then there's an 11 property.

33
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:47,230
Plus, there's an 11th property
that chain them together.

34
00:03:55,950 --> 00:03:58,590
And the last one is
usually referred to

35
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:00,230
as the distributive law.

36
00:04:00,230 --> 00:04:05,790
So that was one of the
two important concepts

37
00:04:05,790 --> 00:04:08,790
that we talked about last time.

38
00:04:08,790 --> 00:04:11,030
The other concept
that we talked about

39
00:04:11,030 --> 00:04:13,060
was what's called
an ordered set.

40
00:04:18,990 --> 00:04:27,030
So that's a set
with an ordering.

41
00:04:27,030 --> 00:04:30,490
And the ordering has to
have the property that--

42
00:04:30,490 --> 00:04:36,990
so this here, the ordering
has the property--

43
00:04:36,990 --> 00:04:38,770
so it has actually
two properties.

44
00:04:47,750 --> 00:04:49,870
The first one is
that whenever you

45
00:04:49,870 --> 00:04:57,460
take an element x and an
element y in the set, then

46
00:04:57,460 --> 00:05:01,060
you should be able to relate
these two by the ordering.

47
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:09,740
So either x is equal
to y or x is smaller

48
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:17,040
than y or y is smaller than x.

49
00:05:21,580 --> 00:05:23,940
And the second
property is what's

50
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:31,420
called transitive, that if x and
y and c are elements in the set

51
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:40,580
and x is less than y and
y is less than c, then

52
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:42,780
x should be less than c.

53
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:45,740
So this is the
transitive property.

54
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:48,300
So the first property,
again, is that you

55
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:50,840
can compare any two elements.

56
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:53,880
And the second one is
this transitive law.

57
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:01,600
So that was the two concepts
we talked about last time.

58
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:07,620
And then we talked about what
is meant to be an ordered field.

59
00:06:07,620 --> 00:06:17,560
So then we also talked
about ordered fields.

60
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:23,160
And then ordered fields
is, you have a field that

61
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:25,440
is also an ordered set.

62
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:33,560
And then the relationship--
so that one being less than

63
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:34,880
the other one--

64
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:37,720
to interact. have So
this is these two things

65
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:41,680
that, if you take
x is less than y

66
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:46,080
and you're adding any
elements on both sides,

67
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:50,350
then it still
preserve the order.

68
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:52,110
So this is one thing.

69
00:06:52,110 --> 00:06:57,310
And the second is that if you
take x that's bigger than 0

70
00:06:57,310 --> 00:07:04,630
and y that's bigger than 0,
then the product of x and y

71
00:07:04,630 --> 00:07:06,990
should be bigger than 0.

72
00:07:06,990 --> 00:07:10,690
And so this is how the--

73
00:07:10,690 --> 00:07:15,670
so an ordered field, again,
is a field that has a set,

74
00:07:15,670 --> 00:07:17,830
also has an ordering.

75
00:07:17,830 --> 00:07:22,750
And then the ordering together
with the operation of the field

76
00:07:22,750 --> 00:07:25,030
should be chained together
by these two rules.

77
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:32,770
So we're getting close
to understanding this.

78
00:07:32,770 --> 00:07:35,370
So this question is--

79
00:07:35,370 --> 00:07:39,750
so in order for all
of this to make sense,

80
00:07:39,750 --> 00:07:45,780
we need to understand what it
means that the real numbers is

81
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:48,060
a complete ordered field.

82
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:51,720
And so we have now this part of
it, that it's an ordered field.

83
00:07:51,720 --> 00:07:53,060
We understand that.

84
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:58,200
This here, we need to
understand what this means.

85
00:07:58,200 --> 00:07:58,700
OK.

86
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:04,380
So now before I get
into that, let's

87
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:15,760
just talk about-- so
what is the difference

88
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:24,460
between the real numbers
and the rational numbers?

89
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:28,420
Remember, the rational numbers
are numbers of this form

90
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:33,085
here, where this is an integer
and this is a natural number.

91
00:08:33,085 --> 00:08:37,460
So what is the difference
between these two?

92
00:08:37,460 --> 00:08:48,200
Well, one difference
is-- so one difference

93
00:08:48,200 --> 00:09:00,120
is that that square root of
2 is a real number but not

94
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:00,970
a rational number.

95
00:09:13,384 --> 00:09:18,650
Now let me try to explain first
why it's not a rational number.

96
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:23,560
So let's try to see--

97
00:09:23,560 --> 00:09:25,260
so what is square root of 2?

98
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:37,080
The square root of 2, you can
think about it as a number, x.

99
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:40,520
And it has to have the property
that if you multiply it

100
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:42,510
by itself--

101
00:09:42,510 --> 00:09:46,550
so x times x or, if
you will, x squared--

102
00:09:46,550 --> 00:09:49,910
this here should be equal to 2.

103
00:09:49,910 --> 00:09:51,710
It also should be positive.

104
00:09:57,950 --> 00:10:04,110
Now suppose-- so I claim
that square root of 2

105
00:10:04,110 --> 00:10:07,010
is not a rational number.

106
00:10:07,010 --> 00:10:09,050
So by that, I mean that--

107
00:10:09,050 --> 00:10:12,170
so I want to claim
the following.

108
00:10:17,110 --> 00:10:40,600
There does not exist a rational
number x equal to m/n, where--

109
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:42,740
again, this is an integer.

110
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:45,240
This is a natural number--

111
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:53,540
so that x squared is equal to 2.

112
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:58,780
So I claim that you don't
have a rational number that

113
00:10:58,780 --> 00:11:00,840
is square root of 2.

114
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:01,340
OK.

115
00:11:01,340 --> 00:11:03,120
Now let's try to prove that.

116
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:23,140
So suppose there
was such a number.

117
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:24,540
So we want to prove it.

118
00:11:24,540 --> 00:11:26,240
So we prove by contradiction.

119
00:11:36,220 --> 00:11:49,180
So assume that there exists
x that is a rational number.

120
00:11:49,180 --> 00:11:50,680
So it can be
written in this form

121
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:59,200
here, where m is an integer,
and n is a natural number.

122
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:03,620
So assume that there exists
an x that is a rational number

123
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:07,080
and so that the
squared is equal to 2.

124
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:10,320
Well, if that's the
case, then m/n--

125
00:12:14,140 --> 00:12:18,280
so this here squared is
supposed to be equal to 2.

126
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:29,720
So this implies that m squared
over n squared is equal to 2.

127
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:36,550
And this, just multiplying over,
implies that m squared is equal

128
00:12:36,550 --> 00:12:40,750
to 2n squared.

129
00:12:40,750 --> 00:12:46,050
Now, when we write x as a
rational number-- of course,

130
00:12:46,050 --> 00:12:48,150
we know that there are
many ways of writing it

131
00:12:48,150 --> 00:12:49,530
as rational number.

132
00:12:49,530 --> 00:12:51,990
Because if you
have one, then you

133
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:56,810
can just multiply the
numerator and denominator

134
00:12:56,810 --> 00:13:01,142
by a natural number, and then
you get another representation.

135
00:13:01,142 --> 00:13:03,190
But we can assume--

136
00:13:03,190 --> 00:13:05,510
so we may assume.

137
00:13:05,510 --> 00:13:07,270
So when we have written--

138
00:13:07,270 --> 00:13:23,700
so we may assume that m and n
does not have a common factor.

139
00:13:27,950 --> 00:13:30,130
Because otherwise, we
could just reduce it.

140
00:13:30,130 --> 00:13:34,230
So we could reduce it until
they don't have a common factor.

141
00:13:44,760 --> 00:13:45,260
OK.

142
00:13:45,260 --> 00:13:49,780
So we will assume that m and
n do not have a common factor.

143
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:53,820
We have now that m
squared is equal to this.

144
00:13:53,820 --> 00:13:59,300
But this means that
m here must be even.

145
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:08,720
So we have that since m squared
is equal to 2n squared--

146
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:13,040
so it means that 2 is
a factor in m squared.

147
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:15,340
So it must actually
be a factor in m.

148
00:14:15,340 --> 00:14:24,580
So it means that 2
here is a factor in m.

149
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:27,980
In other words, m is even.

150
00:14:27,980 --> 00:14:34,946
So you can write m here as 2
times another integer here.

151
00:14:40,730 --> 00:14:48,050
But this means that m squared
is equal to 2 m1 squared, which

152
00:14:48,050 --> 00:14:51,640
is equal to 4 m1 squared.

153
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:56,050
And we already know that
that is equal to 2n squared.

154
00:14:59,170 --> 00:15:03,030
So this means dividing
by 2 on both sides.

155
00:15:03,030 --> 00:15:08,730
It means that 2 m1
squared is equal to n.

156
00:15:08,730 --> 00:15:12,090
But this means that-- sorry,
is equal to n squared.

157
00:15:12,090 --> 00:15:17,650
So this means that 2 here
must be a factor of n.

158
00:15:17,650 --> 00:15:29,290
So this means here that
2 is also a factor in n.

159
00:15:29,290 --> 00:15:33,550
But now you see that
this is a contradiction.

160
00:15:33,550 --> 00:15:35,390
So this is a contradiction.

161
00:15:35,390 --> 00:15:41,190
Because now what we have
is that 2 is a factor in m

162
00:15:41,190 --> 00:15:43,170
and 2 is a factor on n.

163
00:15:43,170 --> 00:15:44,890
So they have a common factor.

164
00:15:44,890 --> 00:15:46,950
But we assumed they didn't
have a common factor.

165
00:15:50,090 --> 00:15:52,430
So we argue by
contradiction-- we assumed

166
00:15:52,430 --> 00:15:55,750
there was a rational number
so that the square of it

167
00:15:55,750 --> 00:15:57,230
was equal to 2.

168
00:15:57,230 --> 00:15:58,990
And we get a contradiction.

169
00:15:58,990 --> 00:16:03,390
So there could not
be a rational number

170
00:16:03,390 --> 00:16:06,850
that has a property that
the square is equal to 2.

171
00:16:06,850 --> 00:16:10,030
So square root of
2 is definitely

172
00:16:10,030 --> 00:16:11,100
not a rational number.

173
00:16:18,150 --> 00:16:25,230
So now, how do we think
about square root of 2?

174
00:16:25,230 --> 00:16:34,910
So how do we fit square root
of 2 into the number system?

175
00:16:47,060 --> 00:16:50,380
Well, square root
of 2 is-- if you

176
00:16:50,380 --> 00:16:55,100
think about it as
a decimal number,

177
00:16:55,100 --> 00:17:09,859
then it starts with 1.4142136.

178
00:17:09,859 --> 00:17:12,460
And then it continue like this.

179
00:17:12,460 --> 00:17:18,020
So one way of thinking
about square root of 2

180
00:17:18,020 --> 00:17:20,220
is that it is--
and we'll come back

181
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:22,460
to this at some later lecture.

182
00:17:22,460 --> 00:17:24,660
So we think about--

183
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:40,330
so one way of thinking
about square root of 2

184
00:17:40,330 --> 00:17:47,960
is as a limit of a
sequence of number.

185
00:17:52,530 --> 00:17:58,530
And that brings us to another
really important concept

186
00:17:58,530 --> 00:18:02,130
that we will talk
much more about later,

187
00:18:02,130 --> 00:18:06,050
is sequences and limits.

188
00:18:06,050 --> 00:18:10,610
And so if we want to think about
it as a limit of a sequence,

189
00:18:10,610 --> 00:18:14,310
we think about maybe
the number 1 first.

190
00:18:14,310 --> 00:18:16,690
And then we think about 1.4.

191
00:18:16,690 --> 00:18:30,830
And then we think 1.4.1, and
then 1.414, and then 1.4142,

192
00:18:30,830 --> 00:18:31,710
et cetera.

193
00:18:31,710 --> 00:18:37,230
And so you see that this here
is a sequence of numbers.

194
00:18:37,230 --> 00:18:44,340
And so this is an increasing
sequence of numbers.

195
00:18:53,950 --> 00:19:02,880
And square root of 2 is
the limit of the sequence.

196
00:19:08,270 --> 00:19:13,830
So again, we'll talk much more
about sequences and limits

197
00:19:13,830 --> 00:19:14,330
later.

198
00:19:14,330 --> 00:19:16,710
But this is just
something to keep in mind

199
00:19:16,710 --> 00:19:23,222
as also motivating why we
need to understand sequences

200
00:19:23,222 --> 00:19:23,930
and their limits.

201
00:19:30,620 --> 00:19:33,940
So we now know--

202
00:19:33,940 --> 00:19:37,980
maybe not super surprisingly,
but we know and see

203
00:19:37,980 --> 00:19:43,980
there was a very simple proof
why the real numbers is larger

204
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:45,420
than the rational numbers.

205
00:19:45,420 --> 00:19:49,100
It contain a particular
square root of 2

206
00:19:49,100 --> 00:19:53,180
that the rational
numbers does not contain.

207
00:19:53,180 --> 00:19:54,480
We still need to--

208
00:19:54,480 --> 00:19:58,500
and this is related to
the completeness of R

209
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:02,520
and why the rational
numbers is not complete.

210
00:20:02,520 --> 00:20:07,220
And this is, again, what
we're now working towards.

211
00:20:07,220 --> 00:20:10,380
So we're working
towards what it means

212
00:20:10,380 --> 00:20:11,970
for a field to be complete.

213
00:20:31,570 --> 00:20:36,330
So let me just write that,
and I'll explain it more.

214
00:20:36,330 --> 00:20:46,290
But this here is what we are
aiming towards, completeness,

215
00:20:46,290 --> 00:20:48,290
which is-- and
I'll explain that--

216
00:20:48,290 --> 00:20:49,800
at the least upper
bound property.

217
00:21:02,708 --> 00:21:03,990
OK.

218
00:21:03,990 --> 00:21:07,110
So suppose you take
an ordered set.

219
00:21:07,110 --> 00:21:15,080
So suppose S is an ordered set.

220
00:21:20,160 --> 00:21:29,280
And the ordering-- as
usual, we're ordering,

221
00:21:29,280 --> 00:21:33,000
we denote by this.

222
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,460
So suppose S is an ordered set.

223
00:21:35,460 --> 00:21:40,880
And suppose that A
here is a subset of S.

224
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:59,328
Then we say that M is an upper
bound for the subset for A

225
00:21:59,328 --> 00:22:15,280
if, for all elements in A, M
here is bigger or equal to A.

226
00:22:15,280 --> 00:22:19,780
So an upper bound
for a subset is

227
00:22:19,780 --> 00:22:22,540
something that has
to be bigger than all

228
00:22:22,540 --> 00:22:25,240
the elements in the
subset, bigger or equal to.

229
00:22:25,240 --> 00:22:27,410
It could be equal to
one of the elements.

230
00:22:30,580 --> 00:22:32,960
So that's a upper bound.

231
00:22:32,960 --> 00:22:36,150
And so, of course,
if you look at--

232
00:22:41,140 --> 00:22:44,820
so a couple of examples here.

233
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:57,580
So if the set here is--
let's say that the set is--

234
00:22:57,580 --> 00:23:01,660
so S is the natural
numbers here.

235
00:23:01,660 --> 00:23:10,240
And the subset here is 1, 2, 3.

236
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:20,770
So then the element 4
here is an upper bound

237
00:23:20,770 --> 00:23:38,690
for A. So 4 is an upper bound
for A, whereas 2 is not.

238
00:23:38,690 --> 00:23:41,090
Obviously, 3 is bigger than 2.

239
00:23:41,090 --> 00:23:43,240
So 2 is not an upper bound.

240
00:23:48,810 --> 00:23:54,170
Another example is if you take--

241
00:23:58,010 --> 00:24:01,290
let's say S is the
rational numbers,

242
00:24:01,290 --> 00:24:07,520
and A here is the
natural numbers.

243
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:18,070
Then A here does not
have an upper bound.

244
00:24:30,240 --> 00:24:37,850
And I'll return to this second
example in just a minute.

245
00:24:44,532 --> 00:24:49,080
s that is what it means
to be an upper bound.

246
00:24:49,080 --> 00:24:54,840
So now there's another concept.

247
00:24:54,840 --> 00:24:56,930
And that is a least upper bound.

248
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:19,330
So there's another concept
of the least upper bound.

249
00:25:26,140 --> 00:25:30,430
So again, we have S
is an ordered set.

250
00:25:35,700 --> 00:25:43,860
We have A here is a subset of S.

251
00:25:43,860 --> 00:25:53,340
Then we say that it's a
subset of S. Maybe let me just

252
00:25:53,340 --> 00:25:55,430
point out that it's bounded.

253
00:26:08,890 --> 00:26:11,420
So And is a subset
of S that is bounded.

254
00:26:14,850 --> 00:26:36,770
Then we say that capital
M is a least upper bound

255
00:26:36,770 --> 00:26:47,570
for a if M is an upper bound--

256
00:26:47,570 --> 00:26:50,730
so it has to be an upper bound--

